You're like I'm gonna snap my leg before I quit this thing.

1028
00:40:48.807 --> 00:40:51.827
Yeah, it was gonna get to the point where

1029
00:40:51.827 --> 00:40:54.940
it was gonna snap and that's what was gonna make-

1030
00:40:54.940 --> 00:40:56.281
It would be the only circumstance

1031
00:40:56.281 --> 00:40:58.410
that would make you to stop.

1032
00:40:58.410 --> 00:41:01.627
It was gonna be the narrative that the public would say,

1033
00:41:01.627 --> 00:41:03.690
okay, that is an acceptable rate.

1034
00:41:03.690 --> 00:41:05.403
Justified.
That's justified

1035
00:41:05.403 --> 00:41:06.676
an acceptable reason to stop.

1036
00:41:06.676 --> 00:41:10.932
Anything short of that is, you're a quitter

1037
00:41:10.932 --> 00:41:12.852
and I obviously don't want that.

1038
00:41:12.852 --> 00:41:15.193
And so that brace saved it

1039
00:41:15.193 --> 00:41:19.743
because it'll literally allow that stress fracture to heal

1040
00:41:19.743 --> 00:41:23.145
while doing a full marathon every single day,

1041
00:41:23.145 --> 00:41:24.765
including the bike miles.

1042
00:41:24.765 --> 00:41:29.765
Now, what came with that was the imbalance that it created,

1043
00:41:30.052 --> 00:41:32.714
which lightning bolted up into my hip,

1044
00:41:32.714 --> 00:41:33.982
which then was the next thing.

1045
00:41:33.982 --> 00:41:36.751
And this project became, like I said,

1046
00:41:36.751 --> 00:41:38.724
injury and management of injury,

1047
00:41:38.724 --> 00:41:43.057
and it was this trickle effect, or what's it called?

1048
00:41:45.506 --> 00:41:47.289
One thing leads to another?
Domino effect.

1049
00:41:47.289 --> 00:41:49.536
Yeah, like a domino effect of injury,

1050
00:41:49.536 --> 00:41:52.356
'cause it started with the ankle, went up into the shin,

1051
00:41:52.356 --> 00:41:53.736
we dealt with that and we figured it out, we're like-

1052
00:41:53.736 --> 00:41:55.433
Then when I moved up through the body.

1053
00:41:55.433 --> 00:41:57.359
And I just remember,

1054
00:41:57.359 --> 00:41:59.239
I was like, I have no shin pain today,

1055
00:41:59.239 --> 00:42:01.609
and we're like, okay, we're clear, we can go,

1056
00:42:01.609 --> 00:42:04.859
and the next day, my hip just exploded,

1057
00:42:05.762 --> 00:42:07.339
like stopped me on my tracks.

1058
00:42:07.339 --> 00:42:08.492
And I remember my therapist, Hayden,

1059
00:42:08.492 --> 00:42:11.676
he was like, can we not get one day

1060
00:42:11.676 --> 00:42:14.068
where we're not dealing with something?

1061
00:42:14.068 --> 00:42:15.331
And it just became this domino effect

1062
00:42:15.331 --> 00:42:18.546
of, I was always dealing with something.

1063
00:42:18.546 --> 00:42:22.097
Like the busted up feet was the relief.

1064
00:42:22.097 --> 00:42:23.899
It was like, oh, good, today you're just missing

1065
00:42:23.899 --> 00:42:26.526
the whole bottom of your foot with skin.

1066
00:42:26.526 --> 00:42:28.543
Solely, this is it today.

1067
00:42:28.543 --> 00:42:30.467
You're looking at this foot thinking,

1068
00:42:30.467 --> 00:42:32.797
how does your foot look like this 85 days in

1069
00:42:32.797 --> 00:42:35.545
yet we're celebrating this because this is all it is.

1070
00:42:35.545 --> 00:42:37.477
Right, right, right, right.

1071
00:42:37.477 --> 00:42:40.510
It's all perspective.
Yeah, yeah.

1072
00:42:40.510 --> 00:42:45.436 line:15%
You told me some crazy stories about the sleep disruption.

1073
00:42:45.436 --> 00:42:46.269 line:15%
Yes.

1074
00:42:46.269 --> 00:42:48.413 line:15%
I mean, that just sounds terrifying.

1075
00:42:48.413 --> 00:42:50.955 line:15%
It was, 'cause outside looking in,

1076
00:42:50.955 --> 00:42:54.250
someone says, oh, okay, he's done the 140 miles today,

1077
00:42:54.250 --> 00:42:56.295
he can now go rest and recover.

1078
00:42:56.295 --> 00:42:58.577
Well, that was the second side of that coin

1079
00:42:58.577 --> 00:42:59.433
or that journey, right?

1080
00:42:59.433 --> 00:43:00.640
So on the outside looking in you're like,

1081
00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:01.525
okay, he's doing the swim,

1082
00:43:01.525 --> 00:43:04.925
the bike, the run 2.4, 112, 26.

1083
00:43:04.925 --> 00:43:06.014
And then they're like, okay-

1084
00:43:06.014 --> 00:43:07.536
The complexities continued.

1085
00:43:07.536 --> 00:43:09.048
Okay, he gets to go home and sleep and rest.

1086
00:43:09.048 --> 00:43:10.294
Well, no, as soon as I got home,

1087
00:43:10.294 --> 00:43:11.847
one, I had to refuel,

1088
00:43:11.847 --> 00:43:13.945
two it's two to three hours

1089
00:43:13.945 --> 00:43:16.485
of pretty intense therapy on the table.

1090
00:43:16.485 --> 00:43:17.565
Yeah, you're on the massage table

1091
00:43:17.565 --> 00:43:19.338
trying to eat, fall asleep.
Immediately trying to eat,

1092
00:43:19.338 --> 00:43:20.417
falling sleep.
While you're eating

1093
00:43:20.417 --> 00:43:22.296
while they're working on body.
Managing problems,

1094
00:43:22.296 --> 00:43:25.343
solving problems, the house is chaos,

1095
00:43:25.343 --> 00:43:27.264
'cause everybody's there, trying to do stuff.

1096
00:43:27.264 --> 00:43:28.936
That's an understatement.
Yeah.

1097
00:43:28.936 --> 00:43:31.462
Yeah, and then once everybody leaves

1098
00:43:31.462 --> 00:43:34.314
and the therapy's done, it was around 11:00 PM, midnight,

1099
00:43:34.314 --> 00:43:36.382
most nights I'd move into my bed,

1100
00:43:36.382 --> 00:43:41.382
and then my second wave of like managing things would start.

1101
00:43:41.835 --> 00:43:45.297
And the three biggest things that we dealt with was

1102
00:43:45.297 --> 00:43:47.743
incredible hot sweats, like we're talking.

1103
00:43:47.743 --> 00:43:49.826
Sweating through duvets

1104
00:43:50.791 --> 00:43:52.874
and I'm like sweating

1105
00:43:52.874 --> 00:43:54.823
like you've never seen, it was wild.

1106
00:43:54.823 --> 00:43:59.436
And then that leads to dehydration problems,

1107
00:43:59.436 --> 00:44:01.922
and trying to keep yourself hydrated.

1108
00:44:01.922 --> 00:44:03.066
Panic attacks.

1109
00:44:03.066 --> 00:44:05.196
Panic attacks, night terrors,

1110
00:44:05.196 --> 00:44:07.194
which finally went away.

1111
00:44:07.194 --> 00:44:09.441
Well, the hyperbaric chamber after a while,

1112
00:44:09.441 --> 00:44:10.506
we're like, you can't sleep in there

1113
00:44:10.506 --> 00:44:12.072
because you can't just get out, right?

1114
00:44:12.072 --> 00:44:14.586
If he's freaking out, we can't get to him,

1115
00:44:14.586 --> 00:44:16.779
he can't get out.

1116
00:44:16.779 --> 00:44:18.011
It was wild.

1117
00:44:18.011 --> 00:44:19.862
And kind of not really just being able

1118
00:44:19.862 --> 00:44:21.988
to get a restful night of sleep, right?

1119
00:44:21.988 --> 00:44:24.531
Like despite the extraordinary fatigue

1120
00:44:24.531 --> 00:44:26.613
and you're laying there on the couch or whatever,

1121
00:44:26.613 --> 00:44:28.611
with NormaTec boots on falling asleep,

1122
00:44:28.611 --> 00:44:31.411
like you're sweating and having the...

1123
00:44:31.411 --> 00:44:34.269
And weren't you doing some sleepwalking too?

1124
00:44:34.269 --> 00:44:35.785
There were some stories.

1125
00:44:35.785 --> 00:44:36.720
Oh, yeah.

1126
00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:38.144
Yeah.
It was great stuff.

1127
00:44:38.144 --> 00:44:39.326
(laughing)

1128
00:44:39.326 --> 00:44:40.159
The kids have some pretty funny stories.

1129
00:44:40.159 --> 00:44:41.847
The kids loved it.

1130
00:44:41.847 --> 00:44:43.270
That was the highlight of the 100 for them.

1131
00:44:43.270 --> 00:44:45.044
I guess there was one time I went upstairs

1132
00:44:45.044 --> 00:44:47.984
to just check on my daughters and I had no idea.

1133
00:44:47.984 --> 00:44:50.889
And one time I was wandering outside the house

1134
00:44:50.889 --> 00:44:52.970
and Dolly was coming home from something

1135
00:44:52.970 --> 00:44:54.235
and she was wondering what I was doing.

1136
00:44:54.235 --> 00:44:56.278
And didn't realize it and they were saying,

1137
00:44:56.278 --> 00:44:57.782
hey, what were you doing outside last night?

1138
00:44:57.782 --> 00:45:00.234
And I was like, I was in my bed last night.

1139
00:45:00.234 --> 00:45:01.682
So these crazy stories of kids-

1140
00:45:01.682 --> 00:45:03.190
Which he has no history of that.

1141
00:45:03.190 --> 00:45:04.271
No recollection of it.
No history of it.

1142
00:45:04.271 --> 00:45:05.176
But it's so interesting,

1143
00:45:05.176 --> 00:45:07.013
like what is going on physiologically

1144
00:45:07.013 --> 00:45:08.998
that would cause that.

1145
00:45:08.998 --> 00:45:10.462
I mean, have you talked to anybody?

1146
00:45:10.462 --> 00:45:12.993
Like you should hook up with like Matt Walker

1147
00:45:12.993 --> 00:45:15.362
and have him figure out like, what was going on with you

1148
00:45:15.362 --> 00:45:18.596
or what precipitated like that kind of strange situation.

1149
00:45:18.596 --> 00:45:22.592
Yeah, and I don't even know how you quantify it

1150
00:45:22.592 --> 00:45:24.788
or like study it now.

1151
00:45:24.788 --> 00:45:29.788
Literally, when I go home tomorrow, I go to a brain MRI,

1152
00:45:30.040 --> 00:45:31.353
'cause I've been dealing with brain fog

1153
00:45:31.353 --> 00:45:32.482
and depression and fatigue

1154
00:45:32.482 --> 00:45:33.705
and things like that.
It's still lingering.

1155
00:45:33.705 --> 00:45:35.491
Yeah, for sure.

1156
00:45:35.491 --> 00:45:37.078
And I have good moments and bad moments

1157
00:45:37.078 --> 00:45:41.341
and it depends on how much like mental energy I have

1158
00:45:41.341 --> 00:45:43.950
to expend throughout a day right now,

1159
00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:46.650
but there'll be a point of the day if I've done a lot,

1160
00:45:46.650 --> 00:45:48.985
I'd be like Sunny, I think I need to just like rest

1161
00:45:48.985 --> 00:45:50.579
or I need you to drive home.

1162
00:45:50.579 --> 00:45:51.704
Right after the fact...

1163
00:45:51.704 --> 00:45:52.602
This is my favorite story.

1164
00:45:52.602 --> 00:45:55.203
Right after the 50, I was driving down the freeway.

1165
00:45:55.203 --> 00:45:57.775
Like at the finish line, leaving the finish line.

1166
00:45:57.775 --> 00:45:59.163
Yeah.
Was I just leaving?

1167
00:45:59.163 --> 00:46:02.181
Yes, because I stuck with you, everybody left.

1168
00:46:02.181 --> 00:46:03.683
We had all this stuff and all these cars

1169
00:46:03.683 --> 00:46:04.668
and no one to drive them home.

1170
00:46:04.668 --> 00:46:06.985
So James had to drive the van home

1171
00:46:06.985 --> 00:46:09.097
from the finish line of the 50

1172
00:46:09.097 --> 00:46:10.355
with all the bikes on the back.

1173
00:46:10.355 --> 00:46:11.749
Oh, I'm thinking of a different story.

1174
00:46:11.749 --> 00:46:13.538
Oh, there more than one.

1175
00:46:13.538 --> 00:46:15.270
I get pulled over by the police on the way home

1176
00:46:15.270 --> 00:46:16.270
from the 50,

1177
00:46:17.644 --> 00:46:19.434
and just had to have an interesting conversation

1178
00:46:19.434 --> 00:46:20.706
with the police, but the one I'm talking about

1179
00:46:20.706 --> 00:46:21.539
was after the 100,

1180
00:46:21.539 --> 00:46:22.995
the 100 was over-
You said after the 50.

1181
00:46:22.995 --> 00:46:24.749
I'm sorry, I meant the 100.

1182
00:46:24.749 --> 00:46:25.972
He's still got brain fog.

1183
00:46:25.972 --> 00:46:27.165
(laughing)

1184
00:46:27.165 --> 00:46:28.492
Which time is it?
This fits perfectly

1185
00:46:28.492 --> 00:46:30.413
to what we're talking about.
Which story?

1186
00:46:30.413 --> 00:46:32.620
I'm driving down the freeway and Sunny looks over to me

1187
00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:35.641
and she goes, "Sweetheart, you're going 45 miles an hour."

1188
00:46:35.641 --> 00:46:38.482
And I was like, oh, okay, I'll pick it up a little bit.

1189
00:46:38.482 --> 00:46:40.165
And he did for five minutes,

1190
00:46:40.165 --> 00:46:43.495
and then I was like, sweetheart, now you're going 50

1191
00:46:43.495 --> 00:46:44.482
and the speed limit is 70.

1192
00:46:44.482 --> 00:46:45.613
I remember saying this is a lot faster

1193
00:46:45.613 --> 00:46:46.809
than we were going on my bike.

1194
00:46:46.809 --> 00:46:47.873
Right.

1195
00:46:47.873 --> 00:46:51.323
But you just slip into these bits of brain fog.

1196
00:46:51.323 --> 00:46:55.002
And so right when I get home, I'm super fortunate.

1197
00:46:55.002 --> 00:46:57.373
Again, one of the only places in the United States

1198
00:46:57.373 --> 00:46:58.331
that does this kind of study

1199
00:46:58.331 --> 00:47:00.914
and testing for concussion and-

1200
00:47:01.998 --> 00:47:03.881
TBI.
Yeah, yeah.

1201
00:47:03.881 --> 00:47:06.375
Post traumatic stuff is in Utah

1202
00:47:06.375 --> 00:47:08.046
and they actually put you into an MRI,

1203
00:47:08.046 --> 00:47:09.856
they set up all the scans

1204
00:47:09.856 --> 00:47:12.343
and then they ask you a series of questions

1205
00:47:12.343 --> 00:47:14.959
and it's designed to light up every part of your brain

1206
00:47:14.959 --> 00:47:16.797
once they take you through the whole protocol.

1207
00:47:16.797 --> 00:47:19.669
And then they can see where blood flow isn't going

1208
00:47:19.669 --> 00:47:21.710
and which pathways are blocked and what's going on,

1209
00:47:21.710 --> 00:47:26.163
and then they put you through an extensive 30 hour protocol

1210
00:47:26.163 --> 00:47:28.227
based on the results that resets

1211
00:47:28.227 --> 00:47:30.834
and repatterns your brain turns it back on,

1212
00:47:30.834 --> 00:47:32.447
and so this is like super exciting for me.

1213
00:47:32.447 --> 00:47:34.490
Like professional, like a lot of football players

1214
00:47:34.490 --> 00:47:35.906
will go there from across the country,

1215
00:47:35.906 --> 00:47:39.523
NFL players or car accidents, concussion people,

1216
00:47:39.523 --> 00:47:41.081
people that are showing like signs of Alzheimer's

1217
00:47:41.081 --> 00:47:42.820
and things like that will go

1218
00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:45.506
to this cognitive therapy place in Provo.

1219
00:47:45.506 --> 00:47:47.145
And I'm just so-

1220
00:47:47.145 --> 00:47:48.135
So you haven't been there yet,

1221
00:47:48.135 --> 00:47:49.818
you're going to go there?
No, right when I land,

1222
00:47:49.818 --> 00:47:52.957
I go from the airport to get those MRI scans

1223
00:47:52.957 --> 00:47:53.986
and then based on the results,

1224
00:47:53.986 --> 00:47:56.968
we'll start the therapy to kind of like reset my brain.

1225
00:47:56.968 --> 00:47:59.738
But when you finished you came down to UCLA,

1226
00:47:59.738 --> 00:48:01.757
like shortly after you completed the 100

1227
00:48:01.757 --> 00:48:03.354
and had a bunch of tests run.

1228
00:48:03.354 --> 00:48:05.356
I mean, what was the upshot of that?

1229
00:48:05.356 --> 00:48:07.729
Yeah, so we had kind of a baseline testing done

1230
00:48:07.729 --> 00:48:09.119
at UCLA prior to,

1231
00:48:09.119 --> 00:48:12.790
and they were mostly looking at like cardiovascular, heart

1232
00:48:12.790 --> 00:48:14.342
lungs, things like that, the size of it,

1233
00:48:14.342 --> 00:48:17.943
what it does under stress, MRI is that way.

1234
00:48:17.943 --> 00:48:18.912
And then they wanted to see,

1235
00:48:18.912 --> 00:48:21.618
okay, if you put your body through

1236
00:48:21.618 --> 00:48:25.827
what the human body can do with 14,000 miles

1237
00:48:25.827 --> 00:48:28.459
and then go and get stress tests done.

1238
00:48:28.459 --> 00:48:30.086
Did we create like heart murmurs?

1239
00:48:30.086 --> 00:48:32.099
Did we create arrhythmic problems?

1240
00:48:32.099 --> 00:48:33.216
Did we create all these things

1241
00:48:33.216 --> 00:48:35.365
that we did damage to the body?

1242
00:48:35.365 --> 00:48:37.373
And as Sunny and I have known,

1243
00:48:37.373 --> 00:48:40.790
the body's an amazing piece of machinery.

1244
00:48:42.311 --> 00:48:45.289
We completely stumped the cardiovascular world

1245
00:48:45.289 --> 00:48:48.159
and these cardiologists, and they were like,

1246
00:48:48.159 --> 00:48:49.043
you're fitter and stronger,

1247
00:48:49.043 --> 00:48:51.754
and you've done zero damage to your heart.

1248
00:48:51.754 --> 00:48:53.343
And did you like break the record

1249
00:48:53.343 --> 00:48:54.176
on the stationary part there?
Yeah, the bike test?

1250
00:48:54.176 --> 00:48:57.237
Yeah, we broke the last record.

1251
00:48:57.237 --> 00:49:01.165
Just 'cause I became such a solid cyclist.

1252
00:49:01.165 --> 00:49:02.201
You do 100.

1253
00:49:02.201 --> 00:49:05.020
And then after a couple of weeks of break,

1254
00:49:05.020 --> 00:49:06.246
you got back on the bike

1255
00:49:06.246 --> 00:49:08.452
and we're crushing some KOMS around.

1256
00:49:08.452 --> 00:49:11.318
Yes, I PRd around our neighborhood

1257
00:49:11.318 --> 00:49:12.503
and some of the climbs that we do,

1258
00:49:12.503 --> 00:49:16.699
and for me, getting out was more mental than anything,

1259
00:49:16.699 --> 00:49:20.414
just because sitting at home and focusing on the fatigue

1260
00:49:20.414 --> 00:49:21.913
and I'm tired and I'm depressed and all that,

1261
00:49:21.913 --> 00:49:24.691
that just magnifies the problem.

1262
00:49:24.691 --> 00:49:26.899
And so for me, getting out...

1263
00:49:26.899 --> 00:49:27.971
I mean the cycling community

1264
00:49:27.971 --> 00:49:29.520
really came out on the 100,

1265
00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:34.520
and so I had developed a lot of really great friendships.

1266
00:49:34.619 --> 00:49:36.504
We'd sit on the bike for six, seven hours a day

1267
00:49:36.504 --> 00:49:38.922
and just talk to these new people.

1268
00:49:38.922 --> 00:49:40.670
The community really came out and said,

1269
00:49:40.670 --> 00:49:42.268
this is our project too, we're protecting you,

1270
00:49:42.268 --> 00:49:43.952
we're gonna get you through this.

1271
00:49:43.952 --> 00:49:45.637
And then a lot of the cyclists

1272
00:49:45.637 --> 00:49:46.740
went through the same thing I did.

1273
00:49:46.740 --> 00:49:48.287
They would come out two, three times a week

1274
00:49:48.287 --> 00:49:49.266
for a quarter of a year

1275
00:49:49.266 --> 00:49:51.989
and then overnight that goes away

1276
00:49:51.989 --> 00:49:54.178
and they're like, well, where's our group rides,

1277
00:49:54.178 --> 00:49:56.670
and where's that comradery, and where is...?

1278
00:49:56.670 --> 00:49:57.503
We've missed that.

1279
00:49:57.503 --> 00:49:59.643
And so for me getting back out, it was great for me,

1280
00:49:59.643 --> 00:50:00.543
it was great for the community,

1281
00:50:00.543 --> 00:50:01.376
it was great for those people

1282
00:50:01.376 --> 00:50:05.126
that had their journey alongside our journey.

1283
00:50:06.236 --> 00:50:07.584
A lot of people are struggling.

1284
00:50:07.584 --> 00:50:09.976
I mean, COVID happened and the world shut down

1285
00:50:09.976 --> 00:50:12.612
and a lot of depression and anxiety happened

1286
00:50:12.612 --> 00:50:15.173
and Utah happened to be one of the states

1287
00:50:15.173 --> 00:50:16.297
from the outside looking in,

1288
00:50:16.297 --> 00:50:19.050
we're like we had still an opportunity to go outside

1289
00:50:19.050 --> 00:50:21.901
and do stuff and it was really great.

1290
00:50:21.901 --> 00:50:24.345
And a lot of those people before...

1291
00:50:24.345 --> 00:50:25.511
We just come out of winter

1292
00:50:25.511 --> 00:50:27.061
and so people have been trapped inside

1293
00:50:27.061 --> 00:50:28.326
and COVID and no families,

1294
00:50:28.326 --> 00:50:29.595
and a lot of people lost jobs.

1295
00:50:29.595 --> 00:50:30.769
And so this was a project

1296
00:50:30.769 --> 00:50:33.843
where it kind of like gave a lot of people, hope and purpose

1297
00:50:33.843 --> 00:50:36.176
alongside of what we were doing

1298
00:50:36.176 --> 00:50:37.109
and that was special.

1299
00:50:37.109 --> 00:50:39.719
This is the real story behind the story.

1300
00:50:39.719 --> 00:50:42.781
There's a lot of focus on how did you do the 100

1301
00:50:42.781 --> 00:50:45.068
and like the particulars of that

1302
00:50:45.068 --> 00:50:48.151
and the family journey that entailed,

1303
00:50:50.338 --> 00:50:52.897
but truly it's the community piece.

1304
00:50:52.897 --> 00:50:56.083
Absolutely.
And it was so impactful

1305
00:50:56.083 --> 00:51:00.389
to visit and see, not only your town come out and support,

1306
00:51:00.389 --> 00:51:02.360
I mean, it was like such a beautiful expression

1307
00:51:02.360 --> 00:51:03.280
of solidarity.

1308
00:51:03.280 --> 00:51:06.693
All these people who were just there for you guys

1309
00:51:06.693 --> 00:51:08.046
in every which way.

1310
00:51:08.046 --> 00:51:09.763
And then on top of that,

1311
00:51:09.763 --> 00:51:12.314
all these people who flew in or drove in

1312
00:51:12.314 --> 00:51:15.217
from all parts of the country, all parts of the world.

1313
00:51:15.217 --> 00:51:17.235
I met people from Eastern Europe

1314
00:51:17.235 --> 00:51:20.817
who flew out to have this experience,

1315
00:51:20.817 --> 00:51:22.552
to share this experience with you,

1316
00:51:22.552 --> 00:51:25.159
people who are inspired by what you were doing

1317
00:51:25.159 --> 00:51:27.113
and what you were sharing online

1318
00:51:27.113 --> 00:51:30.085
and just so many lives transformed.

1319
00:51:30.085 --> 00:51:33.502
And I think that gets to this piece about

1320
00:51:34.857 --> 00:51:37.786
finding the blessings and the misfortunes,

1321
00:51:37.786 --> 00:51:40.748
like the fact that you were compelled to walk

1322
00:51:40.748 --> 00:51:43.701
actually made it more accessible for a lot of people,

1323
00:51:43.701 --> 00:51:46.780
and I think brought a larger contingent of people out.

1324
00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:48.071
That was a tremendous blessing.

1325
00:51:48.071 --> 00:51:50.248
Yeah, yeah, yeah and became something

1326
00:51:50.248 --> 00:51:52.227
that people could wrap their heads around,

1327
00:51:52.227 --> 00:51:54.011
like joining you for a long walk

1328
00:51:54.011 --> 00:51:54.844
every single day.
That part of it

1329
00:51:54.844 --> 00:51:56.549
was so beautiful.
Yeah.

1330
00:51:56.549 --> 00:51:57.730
That part of it.
Yeah, I can't tell you

1331
00:51:57.730 --> 00:51:59.609
how many times every single day,

1332
00:51:59.609 --> 00:52:01.858
one Casey would do kind of a roll count

1333
00:52:01.858 --> 00:52:05.047
on who's here, where are you from?

1334
00:52:05.047 --> 00:52:06.380
And Casey case he did a great job

1335
00:52:06.380 --> 00:52:07.824
at like including everybody talking,

1336
00:52:07.824 --> 00:52:10.715
'cause a lot of days, I wasn't in a good headspace to do it

1337
00:52:10.715 --> 00:52:12.426
and I'm really focused on what I was doing.

1338
00:52:12.426 --> 00:52:13.801
I mean, I spent a lot of time talking to those guys,

1339
00:52:13.801 --> 00:52:16.707
like their job is to protect you, like keep you.

1340
00:52:16.707 --> 00:52:20.493
'Cause you're gonna get some wacky people too,

1341
00:52:20.493 --> 00:52:22.930
who are vying for your attention

1342
00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:24.627
and their job is to like make sure

1343
00:52:24.627 --> 00:52:27.548
that there's a buffer around you so that you...

1344
00:52:27.548 --> 00:52:28.682
Yeah, you can say hello,

1345
00:52:28.682 --> 00:52:30.930
but like you got to focus on what you're trying to do.

1346
00:52:30.930 --> 00:52:33.113
And the amazing part was

1347
00:52:33.113 --> 00:52:35.289
Casey would do the roll call every single day

1348
00:52:35.289 --> 00:52:37.559
and then we'd start to hear about other people's stories

1349
00:52:37.559 --> 00:52:41.624
and one where they came from but then throughout the day,

1350
00:52:41.624 --> 00:52:44.802
that was one of the coolest things for me to experience was,

1351
00:52:44.802 --> 00:52:47.171
people did give me my distance

1352
00:52:47.171 --> 00:52:49.049
and respect what I was going through.

1353
00:52:49.049 --> 00:52:51.466
And yeah, just respected that

1354
00:52:52.646 --> 00:52:57.064
but what was a lot of fun was for five, six hours

1355
00:52:57.064 --> 00:53:00.242
on the walk around, you could hear the chatter

1356
00:53:00.242 --> 00:53:02.575
and the people talking and sharing their stories

1357
00:53:02.575 --> 00:53:03.408
in the subgroups,

1358
00:53:03.408 --> 00:53:07.596
and just people that would have never crossed paths before.

1359
00:53:07.596 --> 00:53:10.304
And every single day without fail,

1360
00:53:10.304 --> 00:53:11.915
we had somebody say, or we heard,

1361
00:53:11.915 --> 00:53:14.817
hey, we wouldn't have been able to participate in this,

1362
00:53:14.817 --> 00:53:16.336
we wouldn't have been able to experience this,

1363
00:53:16.336 --> 00:53:18.003
had James been running.

1364
00:53:19.470 --> 00:53:23.407
And it was just one of the coolest things that happened

1365
00:53:23.407 --> 00:53:27.414
was one, you said community, community was huge.

1366
00:53:27.414 --> 00:53:32.414
But two, my pain and suffering and injury became a blessing

1367
00:53:32.439 --> 00:53:33.794
to so many other people

1368
00:53:33.794 --> 00:53:37.129
that badly wanted to be part of what we were doing,

1369
00:53:37.129 --> 00:53:39.565
but otherwise wouldn't have had an opportunity to do so.

1370
00:53:39.565 --> 00:53:41.272
And we were just talking at lunch

1371
00:53:41.272 --> 00:53:46.208
about this five-year-old kid who had just turned six.

1372
00:53:46.208 --> 00:53:47.770
Do you know this story?

1373
00:53:47.770 --> 00:53:49.043
I don't know.
Jace, little Jace.

1374
00:53:49.043 --> 00:53:50.698
Oh, I do, yeah, but tell us, of course.

1375
00:53:50.698 --> 00:53:52.714
So yeah. it's a really cool story.

1376
00:53:52.714 --> 00:53:56.631
And this kid says, he was a neighbor of Casey's

1377
00:53:57.535 --> 00:53:59.343
and the dad said, okay, we'll go out

1378
00:53:59.343 --> 00:54:00.789
because the kid really wanted to do

1379
00:54:00.789 --> 00:54:02.752
is part of the walk with us.

1380
00:54:02.752 --> 00:54:04.148
And people don't realize,

1381
00:54:04.148 --> 00:54:06.709
we were walking at a pretty decent pace.

1382
00:54:06.709 --> 00:54:10.946
And if we did take a criticism from the elites,

1383
00:54:10.946 --> 00:54:13.812
it was that we were walking and-

1384
00:54:13.812 --> 00:54:16.121
I had to run, I couldn't walk that fast

1385
00:54:16.121 --> 00:54:16.954
even when I jogged.
It was like brisk walk.

1386
00:54:16.954 --> 00:54:18.361
We were walking at a pretty good clip

1387
00:54:18.361 --> 00:54:19.535
and we blew a lot of people.

1388
00:54:19.535 --> 00:54:21.231
I was falling off the back a couple of times

1389
00:54:21.231 --> 00:54:22.848
and I'm like he's moving pretty good,

1390
00:54:22.848 --> 00:54:25.916
like I gotta start running here to catch back up.

1391
00:54:25.916 --> 00:54:29.470
And so there's this little kid just got inspired

1392
00:54:29.470 --> 00:54:30.309
and motivated and said,

1393
00:54:30.309 --> 00:54:33.756
I wanna go do a couple miles with the Iron Cowboy.

1394
00:54:33.756 --> 00:54:35.556
And the dad was like, okay, we'll get your shoes,

1395
00:54:35.556 --> 00:54:38.462
we'll get your shorts on, we'll go out, we'll pick our day.

1396
00:54:38.462 --> 00:54:41.870
And he did, he knocked out three miles with us

1397
00:54:41.870 --> 00:54:44.969
and then his dad's like, okay, we're done, we did the 5k.

1398
00:54:44.969 --> 00:54:46.777
And he's like, no, the Iron Cowboy is still going,

1399
00:54:46.777 --> 00:54:48.912
I wanna keep going with him.

1400
00:54:48.912 --> 00:54:51.656
And you could see him as we started to progress

1401
00:54:51.656 --> 00:54:54.942
and do more miles, he really started to struggle

1402
00:54:54.942 --> 00:54:58.030
but this kid had zero quit in him.

1403
00:54:58.030 --> 00:54:59.087
Just balling, he was just balling.

1404
00:54:59.087 --> 00:55:00.728
He was starting to cry.

1405
00:55:00.728 --> 00:55:01.654
He was in so much pain.

1406
00:55:01.654 --> 00:55:02.538
He was just in so much pain,

1407
00:55:02.538 --> 00:55:06.460
he ended out knocking out 20 miles that night.

1408
00:55:06.460 --> 00:55:07.668
It's crazy.
20 miles.

1409
00:55:07.668 --> 00:55:08.751
And he had to jog

1410
00:55:08.751 --> 00:55:10.611
'cause he's just a little leg, right?

1411
00:55:10.611 --> 00:55:12.995
If you think of a five-year-old kid, right?

1412
00:55:12.995 --> 00:55:15.584
Off the couch, dude, knocks out 20 miles

1413
00:55:15.584 --> 00:55:17.916
and then was just so upset.

1414
00:55:17.916 --> 00:55:19.839
His dad ended up telling us that he was so upset

1415
00:55:19.839 --> 00:55:22.263
that he wasn't able to finish with us

1416
00:55:22.263 --> 00:55:24.438
because he just couldn't go any further.

1417
00:55:24.438 --> 00:55:28.775
And so they plotted out another day and he started before us

1418
00:55:28.775 --> 00:55:30.228
while we were still on the bike

1419
00:55:30.228 --> 00:55:33.164
and he knocked out five or six or seven miles,

1420
00:55:33.164 --> 00:55:34.989
and then we eventually caught him

1421
00:55:34.989 --> 00:55:36.359
and he stayed with us the rest of the time.

1422
00:55:36.359 --> 00:55:38.417
And this five, six year old kid ended up doing

1423
00:55:38.417 --> 00:55:40.596
a full marathon with us,

1424
00:55:40.596 --> 00:55:43.479
and it was just one of the most unbelievable experiences.

1425
00:55:43.479 --> 00:55:47.807
And we were with someone at lunch and they had just said,

1426
00:55:47.807 --> 00:55:50.557
he hadn't been taught yet to quit

1427
00:55:52.317 --> 00:55:54.194
and it wasn't part of his culture.

1428
00:55:54.194 --> 00:55:55.905
And so like, it begs the question,

1429
00:55:55.905 --> 00:56:00.405
like when in our adult journey is that a taught thing?

1430
00:56:02.035 --> 00:56:03.659
When did it become acceptable?

1431
00:56:03.659 --> 00:56:05.937
When did that be part of our culture?

1432
00:56:05.937 --> 00:56:08.953
'Cause that kid was just like, no, I'm in a lot of pain,

1433
00:56:08.953 --> 00:56:13.275
but I want to do this and I'm going to endure

1434
00:56:13.275 --> 00:56:14.415
until I finish.
I'm telling you,

1435
00:56:14.415 --> 00:56:15.295
he was balling.

1436
00:56:15.295 --> 00:56:16.978
The last three rounds, just crying

1437
00:56:16.978 --> 00:56:18.535
and just kept going, it was amazing.

1438
00:56:18.535 --> 00:56:21.148
And crying with a smile on his face

1439
00:56:21.148 --> 00:56:22.479
and just push and pushing through,

1440
00:56:22.479 --> 00:56:23.815
was really, really special.
And for his father

1441
00:56:23.815 --> 00:56:25.720
to allow him.

1442
00:56:25.720 --> 00:56:27.766
You know a lot of parents are swinging in

1443
00:56:27.766 --> 00:56:30.966
and rescuing this kid and Sean, let him.

1444
00:56:30.966 --> 00:56:32.607
Sean let him fight through those tears,

1445
00:56:32.607 --> 00:56:33.714
Sean, let him finish.

1446
00:56:33.714 --> 00:56:35.759
Like that's as beautiful of a story as it is

1447
00:56:35.759 --> 00:56:37.613
that Jace get it done.

1448
00:56:37.613 --> 00:56:40.130
Yeah and his sense of personal possibility now

1449
00:56:40.130 --> 00:56:42.719
is very different than it was before that.

1450
00:56:42.719 --> 00:56:44.383
But that's just why, I mean, he's the youngest,

1451
00:56:44.383 --> 00:56:47.038
but there were so many people who showed up

1452
00:56:47.038 --> 00:56:48.791
who had never done anything before

1453
00:56:48.791 --> 00:56:52.416
or were coming off of crazy weight loss stories

1454
00:56:52.416 --> 00:56:53.249
and all kinds of stuff.

1455
00:56:53.249 --> 00:56:54.633
There's a lot of first marathons.

1456
00:56:54.633 --> 00:56:58.797
Yeah, I think the power in what you were doing

1457
00:56:58.797 --> 00:57:01.457
was that it normalize something

1458
00:57:01.457 --> 00:57:04.594
that seems impossible to most people,

1459
00:57:04.594 --> 00:57:07.074
and just to see you doing it day after day after day

1460
00:57:07.074 --> 00:57:10.474
and the walking piece made it accessible to people.

1461
00:57:10.474 --> 00:57:12.709
And so they're like, well, maybe I'll show up

1462
00:57:12.709 --> 00:57:13.964
and I'll do a little bit

1463
00:57:13.964 --> 00:57:15.436
and then they end up doing a little bit longer

1464
00:57:15.436 --> 00:57:16.443
than they thought they would.

1465
00:57:16.443 --> 00:57:18.341
And then before, oh, I'll come tomorrow and...

1466
00:57:18.341 --> 00:57:20.012
And then they're doing like the whole marathon,

1467
00:57:20.012 --> 00:57:21.984
and there are people who are doing the full iron man?

1468
00:57:21.984 --> 00:57:23.481
17, 18 folds.
Yeah like people

1469
00:57:23.481 --> 00:57:27.391
who would never sign up for an official iron man showing up

1470
00:57:27.391 --> 00:57:29.434
and doing the full thing

1471
00:57:29.434 --> 00:57:31.400
because they see you doing it every single day,

1472
00:57:31.400 --> 00:57:33.327
and suddenly it seems maybe not as hard

1473
00:57:33.327 --> 00:57:34.671
as they thought it might be.

1474
00:57:34.671 --> 00:57:37.754
We just sent out hundreds of shirts

1475
00:57:39.582 --> 00:57:40.959
because what we said to people is like we said,

1476
00:57:40.959 --> 00:57:42.683
hey, come join us,

1477
00:57:42.683 --> 00:57:46.683
and if you do, all swim, all bike, all run with us,

1478
00:57:46.683 --> 00:57:48.323
when this is over, we'll send everybody

1479
00:57:48.323 --> 00:57:50.043
that finished a finisher shirt.

1480
00:57:50.043 --> 00:57:52.435
And they finally came in and we literally...

1481
00:57:52.435 --> 00:57:54.850
I mean, Lucy is probably still packaging them up right now,

1482
00:57:54.850 --> 00:57:57.216
but we've sent out hundreds of...

1483
00:57:57.216 --> 00:57:59.930
We did the full triathlon.
We did the full with us

1484
00:57:59.930 --> 00:58:01.363
hundreds, hundreds of shirts.

1485
00:58:01.363 --> 00:58:03.414
We had four full boxes of shirts.

1486
00:58:03.414 --> 00:58:04.742
I mean, we ran out of bags

1487
00:58:04.742 --> 00:58:06.982
and I counted how many shirts we didn't have bags for

1488
00:58:06.982 --> 00:58:08.570
and it was 50.
Wow.

1489
00:58:08.570 --> 00:58:10.396
And that was after we ran out of shipping bags.

1490
00:58:10.396 --> 00:58:11.642
Yeah, yeah.

1491
00:58:11.642 --> 00:58:13.143
And so that many people

1492
00:58:13.143 --> 00:58:16.405
and most of them, it was their first, like they just...

1493
00:58:16.405 --> 00:58:18.227
And we had people that would show up and like,

1494
00:58:18.227 --> 00:58:20.735
okay, day 15, they're gonna do part of the bike ride

1495
00:58:20.735 --> 00:58:22.209
and see how the pacing goes

1496
00:58:22.209 --> 00:58:24.167
and then they showed up and did part of the run.

1497
00:58:24.167 --> 00:58:26.935
Or they would see who was finishing the full triathlon

1498
00:58:26.935 --> 00:58:30.155
and they were like, if that guy can do it, I can do it.

1499
00:58:30.155 --> 00:58:31.194
If that girl can...

1500
00:58:31.194 --> 00:58:35.142
I mean, Clint, a 15 year old kid came out and did,

1501
00:58:35.142 --> 00:58:38.216
had no like triathlon background at all.

1502
00:58:38.216 --> 00:58:39.837
He had no cleats on his bike shoes

1503
00:58:39.837 --> 00:58:42.274
Yeah, he bought a bike two weeks before or something

1504
00:58:42.274 --> 00:58:43.220
and he'd gotten out.
He was falling over

1505
00:58:43.220 --> 00:58:45.467
at intersection, he was so exhausted.

1506
00:58:45.467 --> 00:58:48.378
He guided it out.
It was so fun to see

1507
00:58:48.378 --> 00:58:49.211
these young kids
Then he rallied

1508
00:58:49.211 --> 00:58:50.544
and was bringing friends in.

1509
00:58:50.544 --> 00:58:52.926
And Hunter Sweating, he's just graduated

1510
00:58:52.926 --> 00:58:56.150
and he's Lucy's age, and he's had a really rough upbringing

1511
00:58:56.150 --> 00:58:59.582
and he lost 70 pounds, almost doing 75 par

1512
00:58:59.582 --> 00:59:01.339
and this was his last final project.

1513
00:59:01.339 --> 00:59:02.701
He did a full triathlon

1514
00:59:02.701 --> 00:59:05.268
and he finished it two o'clock in the morning,

1515
00:59:05.268 --> 00:59:06.956
well after the group had finished.

1516
00:59:06.956 --> 00:59:09.911
And what about the, I forget his name, the Hispanic guy?

1517
00:59:09.911 --> 00:59:10.986
Takashi?

1518
00:59:10.986 --> 00:59:12.904
Yeah, oh right, yeah.
I loved Takashi.

1519
00:59:12.904 --> 00:59:15.662
Yeah, he never swam, never biked, never ran,

1520
00:59:15.662 --> 00:59:16.832
got it out that full thing.

1521
00:59:16.832 --> 00:59:18.429
He just posted a picture, he's proudest can be

1522
00:59:18.429 --> 00:59:19.959
with his conquered shirt on.

1523
00:59:19.959 --> 00:59:21.609
He's the most delightful person on this planet.

1524
00:59:21.609 --> 00:59:23.143
And Seif?

1525
00:59:23.143 --> 00:59:24.032
How do you say his name?

1526
00:59:24.032 --> 00:59:26.633
Seif, yeah, Seif, oh, Seif.
Seif, Seif

1527
00:59:26.633 --> 00:59:28.431
Yeah Seif.
Yeah, from India.

1528
00:59:28.431 --> 00:59:31.450
India by way of Dallas, like a PhD student

